-- Adds 1 new Module 3 lesson: structured output / JSON mode. Foundational for
-- anyone actually building real applications with AI (parsing reliable data
-- vs. free text), covered in every serious prompting/API course, and
-- directly connects to how tool-calling works under the hood (Module 6).
--
-- Anchored to a lesson TITLE via subquery, safe regardless of what other
-- renumbering has already run. Placed as the new close of Module 3, after
-- "Chain-of-Thought: Getting AI to Show Its Work".

UPDATE public.lessons SET order_index = -order_index
WHERE order_index > (SELECT order_index FROM public.lessons WHERE module_number = 3 AND title = 'Chain-of-Thought: Getting AI to Show Its Work');
UPDATE public.lessons SET order_index = -order_index + 1 WHERE order_index < 0;

WITH new_lesson AS (
  INSERT INTO public.lessons (module_number, order_index, title, slug, summary, coin_cost, is_published)
  VALUES (
    3,
    (SELECT order_index FROM public.lessons WHERE module_number = 3 AND title = 'Chain-of-Thought: Getting AI to Show Its Work') + 1,
    'Structured Output: Getting AI to Speak JSON',
    'structured-output-json-mode',
    'Why real applications ask AI for a strict, predictable shape instead of free-form prose.',
    10,
    true
  )
  RETURNING id
), ins_content AS (
  INSERT INTO public.lesson_content (lesson_id, content)
  SELECT id, '{"hook":"A model that writes a beautiful paragraph is useless to your code if you need one exact number out of it. Structured output fixes that.","explanation":"Most of what you''ve prompted so far asks for free-form text — a paragraph, an explanation, a reply. But real applications usually need to DO something with the model''s answer in code: store it, display it, feed it to another function. Free-form text is hard to parse reliably — does the model say “the answer is 42” or “I think it''s 42” or just “42.”? Structured output solves this by asking the model to respond in a strict, predictable format — almost always JSON — matching an exact schema you define upfront. Instead of “tell me about this product,” you ask for a specific shape like {name, price, in_stock} and specify that ONLY that JSON should be returned, nothing else. Your code can then parse it directly and reliably, every time, instead of trying to extract information from unpredictable prose. Most modern LLM APIs even support a dedicated JSON mode or schema parameter that guarantees valid, parseable JSON as the response — removing almost all the guesswork.","analogy":"Asking a friend to describe a recipe in a sentence versus handing them a form with exact blanks: Dish Name: ___, Prep Time: ___, Ingredients: ___. The form guarantees you get information in a shape you can actually use — no guessing where one field ends and the next begins.","fun_fact":"Structured output is exactly how a chatbot “calls a tool” under the hood — the model doesn''t literally run code, it outputs a JSON object naming the tool and its arguments, and YOUR code parses that JSON and actually runs the function.","try_it":"Pick a task that currently returns free text from an AI you''ve used (like “summarize this article”). Design a JSON schema with 2-3 fields that would make that response actually usable inside a real app.","code":"# FREE-FORM: hard to reliably parse in code\nfree_form_response = \"The product is called SolarLamp Pro, it costs $34.99, and yes, it''s currently in stock.\"\n\n# STRUCTURED: the model is asked to return exactly this shape\nstructured_response = ''{\"name\": \"SolarLamp Pro\", \"price\": 34.99, \"in_stock\": true}''\n\nimport json\n\n# Free-form requires fragile string-searching to extract anything useful.\n# Structured just parses directly, every time.\ndata = json.loads(structured_response)\nprint(f\"Name: {data[''name'']}\")\nprint(f\"Price: ${data[''price'']}\")\nprint(f\"In stock: {data[''in_stock'']}\")\n\n# The SCHEMA you''d actually give the model in the prompt\nschema = {\n    \"name\": \"string\",\n    \"price\": \"number\",\n    \"in_stock\": \"boolean\",\n}\nprompt = f\"Extract product details as JSON matching this schema: {schema}\\nProduct info: SolarLamp Pro costs $34.99 and is in stock.\"\nprint(prompt)","visual":{"caption":"From unpredictable prose to code your program can actually use","steps":[{"emoji":"📝","label":"Free-Form Text","caption":"Hard to parse reliably — wording varies every time."},{"emoji":"📐","label":"Define a Schema","caption":"Decide exactly which fields you need, and their types."},{"emoji":"🔒","label":"Ask for JSON Only","caption":"Instruct the model to return ONLY that structure."},{"emoji":"📦","label":"Structured Data Back","caption":"A predictable shape, every single time."},{"emoji":"⚙️","label":"Code Parses It Directly","caption":"No guessing, no fragile text-searching."}]},"practice":{"prompt":"Why is structured (JSON) output more reliable for a real application than free-form text?","options":["Code can parse it directly and predictably every time, instead of guessing where information starts or ends in prose","JSON responses are always shorter than free-form text","It makes the model respond faster","It has no real advantage over free-form text"],"correct_index":0,"feedback":"A predictable, defined shape is exactly what lets your code reliably extract and use the model’s answer, instead of fragile string-searching through prose."},"code_practice":{"instructions":"This JSON string needs to be parsed into a real Python dictionary before its fields can be used. Complete the missing line.","starter":"import json\n\nresponse = ''{\"title\": \"The Great Gatsby\", \"author\": \"F. Scott Fitzgerald\"}''\n\n# TODO: parse the JSON string into a Python dict called data\n\n\nprint(data[\"title\"])\nprint(data[\"author\"])","check_pattern":"data\\s*=\\s*json\\.loads\\(","success_message":"That''s it -- json.loads() turns a JSON string into a real Python dict you can index directly, exactly what makes structured output actually usable in code.","hint":"Add: data = json.loads(response)"}}'::jsonb FROM new_lesson
  RETURNING lesson_id
)
INSERT INTO public.lesson_quiz_questions (lesson_id, order_index, question, options, correct_index, explanation)
SELECT ins_content.lesson_id, q.order_index, q.question, q.options::jsonb, q.correct_index, q.explanation
FROM ins_content, (VALUES
  (1, 'What problem does structured output solve?', '["Free-form text is unpredictable and hard for code to reliably parse","It makes the model generate responses faster","It removes the need for prompts entirely","It prevents the model from ever making mistakes"]', 0, 'Structured output guarantees a predictable shape, so code can extract information reliably instead of guessing at prose wording.'),
  (2, 'What format is structured output almost always requested in?', '["JSON","A random string of numbers","Plain unstructured sentences","Binary machine code"]', 0, 'JSON is the standard format because it is both human-readable and trivially parseable by virtually every programming language.'),
  (3, 'What does a "schema" define, in the context of structured output?', '["The exact fields you need back, and their types","The model’s internal weights","The temperature setting to use","The number of tokens the response must use"]', 0, 'A schema is the agreed-upon shape of the response — which fields exist and what type each one is.'),
  (4, 'In the code example, why is json.loads() used on structured_response?', '["To convert the JSON string into a real Python dictionary that can be indexed by field name","To convert the dictionary back into a string","To delete the response entirely","To make the code run slower on purpose"]', 0, 'json.loads() parses a JSON string into a native Python dict, letting you access fields like data["name"] directly.'),
  (5, 'How is structured output related to how a chatbot "calls a tool"?', '["The model outputs a JSON object naming the tool and its arguments, which your code then parses and executes","Tool calling has nothing to do with structured output","The model literally runs the tool’s code itself, with no JSON involved","Tool calling only works with free-form text"]', 0, 'Tool/function calling is a specific application of structured output — the model returns a JSON description of what to call, and your code does the actual execution.'),
  (6, 'A team asks a model to "describe this product" with no schema, then tries to extract the price using text search on the reply. What problem are they likely to hit?', '["The wording varies each time, making reliable extraction fragile and error-prone","This approach always works perfectly with zero issues","JSON parsing will fail because no JSON was requested","The model will refuse to answer the question at all"]', 0, 'Without a defined structure, the exact phrasing of the price can vary run to run, breaking simple text-search extraction.'),
  (7, 'What do many modern LLM APIs offer to make structured output more reliable?', '["A dedicated JSON mode or schema parameter that guarantees valid, parseable JSON","A way to disable structured output entirely","A feature that only works for image generation","Nothing — structured output cannot be made more reliable"]', 0, 'Many APIs let you specify a schema directly, which the model is constrained to follow, removing most of the guesswork from free-form prompting.')
) AS q(order_index, question, options, correct_index, explanation);
